<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Pacajes</h1><p class="st-meta">1150 CE – 1450 CE (approx.) · capital Caquiaviri</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Aymara kingdom on the southern shore of Lake Titicaca whose territory included Tiwanaku and later became a key Spanish province.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Pacajes, or Pakasa, formed one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aymara">Aymara</a> kingdoms that emerged around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a> after Tiwanaku's collapse, occupying the high plain south of the lake in modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Bolivia</a> from the 12th century. Their capital was Caquiaviri.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Like other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aymara">Aymara</a> kingdoms, Pacajes was divided into two halves, Urcosuyu and Umasuyu, each with its own lords. Its economy was based on herding llamas and alpacas and farming tubers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a>, freeze-dried into chuño. Pacajes lords built chullpa burial towers and kept colonies in warmer valleys to the east.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Pacajes was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a> in the mid-15th century. Under Spanish rule it became a province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/royal-audiencia-of-charcas">Royal Audiencia of Charcas</a>, and its people supplied labour for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> mines under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">mita</a>. Caquiaviri retains a notable colonial church.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/pacajes.jpg" alt="Pacajes" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Daan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bolivia_La_Paz_Pacajes.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1150 CE – 1450 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Caquiaviri</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Local cults, ancestor worship</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aymara</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/bolivia">Bolivia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pakasa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1300&amp;focus=pacajes" aria-label="Pacajes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2813 2827 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Pacajes lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3133 3083L3027 2937"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3027 2937h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3133 3083h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3133 3083h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1300&amp;focus=pacajes">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

